API patterns (also called API design patterns) are standard ways to design, expose, and consume APIs so they are scalable, maintainable,
and easy to use. Since you’re a Java backend developer, I’ll explain them in a clear, interview-oriented way with examples.

1. REST (Representational State Transfer)

📌 Most common API pattern

Key ideas

Resource-based URLs

Uses HTTP methods

Stateless

Example

GET    /users/10
POST   /users
PUT    /users/10
DELETE /users/10


HTTP methods meaning

GET → read

POST → create

PUT → update (full)

PATCH → update (partial)

DELETE → remove

✅ Simple
✅ Cacheable
❌ Over-fetching / under-fetching sometimes

Used in: Spring Boot, Microservices

2. CRUD Pattern

📌 Built on REST, focused on database operations

Operation	HTTP Method
Create	POST
Read	GET
Update	PUT / PATCH
Delete	DELETE

Example

POST /orders
GET /orders/123


✅ Easy to understand
❌ Not good for complex workflows

3. RPC (Remote Procedure Call)

📌 API looks like calling a function

Example

POST /calculateTax
POST /sendOtp


Payload

{
  "amount": 1000
}


✅ Simple logic mapping
❌ Not resource-oriented

Used in: Legacy systems, internal services

4. SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol)

📌 XML-based, strict contract

Characteristics

Uses WSDL

Strong typing

Heavy XML

✅ Secure
❌ Slow, verbose

Used in: Banks, legacy enterprise systems

5. GraphQL

📌 Client decides what data it needs

Example

{
  user(id: 10) {
    name
    email
  }
}


✅ No over-fetching
✅ Single endpoint
❌ Complex caching

Used in: Modern frontend-heavy apps

6. Event-Driven API (Async Pattern)

📌 Communication via events

Example

OrderCreated → Kafka Topic → Billing Service


✅ Highly scalable
✅ Loose coupling
❌ Harder to debug

Used in: Microservices, Kafka, RabbitMQ

7. Webhooks

📌 Server calls client on event

Example

Payment success → POST /webhook/payment


✅ Real-time updates
❌ Retry handling needed

8. API Gateway Pattern

📌 Single entry point for multiple services

Responsibilities

Authentication

Rate limiting

Logging

Routing

Flow

Client → API Gateway → Microservices


Tools

Spring Cloud Gateway

Kong

NGINX

9. Backend-for-Frontend (BFF)

📌 Separate backend per frontend

Example

Mobile BFF
Web BFF


✅ Optimized responses
❌ More services to maintain

10. HATEOAS

📌 API responses contain links

Example

{
  "id": 10,
  "links": {
    "self": "/users/10",
    "orders": "/users/10/orders"
  }
}


✅ Discoverable APIs
❌ Rarely used in practice

11. Pagination, Filtering & Sorting Pattern

📌 Handles large datasets

Example

GET /users?page=1&size=20&sort=name

12. Versioning Pattern

📌 Prevents breaking clients

Common approaches

/api/v1/users
/api/v2/users


or

Accept: application/vnd.app.v1+json

Interview Tip (Important ⭐)

If interviewer asks:

Which API pattern do you prefer and why?

Answer:

“I mostly use RESTful APIs with proper HTTP methods, pagination, versioning, and API Gateway for microservices. For async communication, I prefer event-driven APIs using Kafka.”